    "body": "A service launched Nov. 9 promises to make donating excess inventory cheaper than destroying it by finding nonprofit recipients and arranging transportation . Vessel, the New York startup behind the service, also announced $5 million in seed funding led by HG Ventures.\nWhat’s Related\nThe company says brands including Sol de Janeiro and Smashbox have already used it to donate more than $83 million in products to more than 5.5 million people. It reports keeping more than 2,000 tons of goods out of landfills or incineration while working before its public launch.\nVessel handles recipient screening, freight booking, shipment tracking and donation records. Brands approve the organizations receiving their products and use money already budgeted for destruction to pay for the service.\n“We built Vessel so that brands no longer have to choose between doing what’s right for their business and what consumers expect,” said Teddy Raskin, Vessel’s co-founder and CEO.\nIt starts with a spreadsheet\nCompanies start by sending a spreadsheet or product list so Vessel can check what is suitable for donation. Its GoodOS software uses AI to assess more than 550,000 nonprofits across more than 160 million data points, then human reviewers review them before an organization earns Vessel Certified status.\nCustomers review the proposed recipients, approve the quote and prepare the goods for pickup. Vessel coordinates with the warehouse and receiving organizations, with records available through a dashboard.\nThose records include bills of lading, signed proof of delivery and charity acknowledgment letters. Vessel says its standard nonprofit terms prohibit resale, and it puts product-use rules in writing before the customer approves a donation.\nWhat the cost guarantee covers\nAccording to Vessel’s website, qualifying inventory can be donated for at least 10% less than the customer’s total destruction cost. That comparison includes Vessel’s fees and freight and does not depend on tax savings.\nTiming depends on the products, recipients’ ability to accept them, and freight availability. Vessel reviews those factors before confirming a plan.\nCheaper than destruction\nVessel says an unnamed medical distributor redirected more than 16 million syringes, totaling over 700 pallets , to more than 20 animal-care nonprofits across six states. The company said the project cost substantially less than destruction.\nIt also worked with an unnamed food and agricultural company to turn excess raw materials into more than 250,000 bars of soap for hygiene programs in Tanzania, Nepal and Madagascar. Money previously allocated for destruction paid for that project.\nFlex Capital, Ford Street Ventures, and I2BF Global Ventures also participated in the seed round.",
